## Assembly and runtime output

`CompiledProject` is the builder-facing view of successful compiler and graph
results. Its entries, package surface, metadata and resources stay paired with
the compilation that owns them. Handles from one compilation cannot be resolved
against another compilation's similarly numbered objects.

### Choose an assembly, not a compiler stage

Use `entries` to inspect selected executable entries and `require_package` when
the builder requires a project facade. Builder-defined callable roots are
selected through their explicit purpose or binding contract. The build driver
computes their reachable functions, generated instances, resource uses and
runtime requirements from compiler-owned facts.

The builder doesn't guess entry status from a filename, nonempty HIR or the
presence of a template. An API-only support module remains API-only. Importing
a module exposes its legal declarations without activating dormant root work.
A Moth package export isn't automatically a native host export, foreign
component interface or network endpoint.

Use `BuildContext` lowering services for those assemblies. A request to
`compile_wasm` asks for validated Wasm output, including its runtime contract.
A browser assembly uses the browser compilation service and its JS/Wasm policy.
The service rejects unsupported reachable capabilities before emission.

These services own the compiler handoff. They arrange link-level lifetime
validation, capability checks, target partitioning and physical memory planning
before lowering. The builder supplies its lifecycle and output policies but
never selects a memory strategy, manufactures call summaries or repackages
numeric proof tables.

### Read constant and runtime templates

An entry's template assembly view exposes folded fragments, runtime fragment
positions and the executable entry contract. It contains no AST or template-IR
handles.

A constant top-level template is already a folded string value. It may still
contain structural resource or site-root pieces whose final characters depend
on output placement. Use the context's structural-string rendering service to
resolve those pieces. Plain-text extraction is valid only when every piece has
concrete characters.

A runtime template is executable Moth code that produces a string. It isn't a
second build-time template evaluator. Runtime top-level templates contribute to
the normal entry's fragment result collection.

Each folded fragment records how many runtime fragments precede it. For a
constant fragment A, a runtime fragment B and another constant fragment C, the
assembly records:

[codeblock, $code("text"):
    A: before runtime position 0
    B: runtime position 0
    C: before runtime position 1
]

The shared assembly service interleaves the folded strings and successful
runtime results in source order. It doesn't need to recover their positions
from rendered HTML or executable IR.

A direct `.mtf` compilation uses the named template service when only folded
content is needed. That service doesn't run a full backend pipeline or invent a
page URL context. It returns structural content and the retained facts the
builder needs to place its resources.

### Consume the entry outcome

A normal executable entry's generated `start` has built-in `Error!` alongside
its fragment-collection success slots. The generated runtime caller invokes it
once for that entry activation and branches on the typed outcome.

Only success publishes that invocation's runtime fragments. On error, discard
unpublished runtime results and leave existing static content in place. Read
neither undefined success slots nor a partial fragment list as success. The
failure is a program execution result, not a failed compiler invocation.

Fragment staging isn't rollback. Earlier explicit IO, host calls and DOM
changes remain done. The host doesn't retry startup, continue later statements
or turn an error into a successful empty page. Error code zero remains an error
when it arrives on the error channel.

An embedding host delivers the failure to its caller. A terminal host reports
failure and uses a nonzero exit status independently of the error code. A web
host uses its application-safe failure presentation instead of inserting raw
error messages as HTML. Assertions and unexpected startup faults remain
distinct from the declared error result. The wrapper owns this invocation,
not every later callback or unrelated script.

A wholly static page doesn't acquire mandatory application runtime code just
because the entry contract has an error channel. Reuse and runtime emission
remain reachability-driven.

### Declare lifecycles without fixing the Wasm engine

A builder's consumer contract supplies lifecycle roots such as an entry,
request, frame or host-owned arena. These tell the compiler which execution
lifetimes apply. They don't weaken source ownership rules or allow retained
values to escape invalid lifetimes.

A lifecycle root is not an instruction to create one Wasm instance. Hosts can
use separately created, pooled or longer-lived instances when they preserve
isolation, valid access and required cleanup. Persistent host resources and
request-scoped context need explicit owners even when the compiled module bytes
are reused.

The Rust host installs typed imports and executes compiled Moth through a
compatible runtime adapter. Wasmer, Wasmtime and other engine choices belong to
the embedding integration, not to Moth source semantics. The adapter validates
the artefact's host contract before calling an export. Sharing source between
browser and server doesn't share memory or runtime authority.

### Plan resources before materialising them

Resource origins, byte sources, destination paths and rendered URLs are
different facts. The compiler retains semantic origins and authored uses. The
builder supplies output placement and URL context. The central output system
owns reads, writes, conflicts and manifests.

Use output and resource planning services to reserve the consuming artefact
and its context before lowering any URL-dependent code. A page URL context is
the document, while a standalone stylesheet uses its own location. A builder
that cannot supply a valid context reports an unsupported use rather than
guessing a root slash or treating a resource reference as ordinary text.

All output destinations are preflighted before ordinary resource bytes are
read or hashed. Unused resources may remain tracked inputs without being read
or emitted. The build system deduplicates compatible resource origins and
rejects conflicting output owners. Runtime assets, source resources and builder
artefacts use the same ownership rules.

`BuildArtifacts` records the result. The output writer validates relative paths
and configured-root containment, retains manifest ownership and removes only
stale files owned by the corresponding successful build. A builder cannot
compose with another builder by silently overwriting its outputs.

### Preserve failure categories

The SDK preserves the difference between a source diagnostic, an operational
build failure and a compiler bug. Authored locations and typed reasons travel
with diagnostics through the public reporting view. Builder code doesn't need
the compiler's interner tables to produce a useful report.

Runtime `moth::host::Error` values and entry failures belong to execution, not
to `moth::builder::Error` as an alternative compiler diagnostic. Rust adapter
faults also don't acquire a typed Moth error channel that the binding never
declared. The host's fault policy handles them at its owned execution boundary.

### Test the boundary through the public API

Compile representative projects through `build_project` and inspect their
artefacts and diagnostics. Exercise the same definition and assembly services
used by the application rather than constructing HIR or fake compiler results.
Use temporary output roots when testing writing and cleanup.

A builder's tests should cover successful registration and duplicate rejection,
unsupported capabilities, required configuration, const/runtime fragment
ordering, startup success and error, resource-context failures and output
ownership conflicts.

Host-binding tests also cover Rust signatures rejected at compile time and
actual calls through the selected runtime adapter. Verify malformed values,
non-finite float results, stale or foreign resource handles, conflicting mutable
access and missing host context. Descriptor inspection alone doesn't test ABI
conversion or runtime lifetime safety.

Keep the host package interface and installed implementations consistent across
build and runtime tests. A function that type-checks in Moth still needs a valid
implementation in the execution environment that will call it.
